  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Bags or a truck?</h2>
    <p>1 cubic yard is 27 cubic feet, which is 45 bags of 80 lb mix: 3,600 lb to haul, open, and mix by hand. That is roughly where bags stop making sense for most people. The walkway in the table is a reasonable bag job. The 12 × 12 patio at 89 bags is not, unless you rent a mixer and have help.</p>
    <p>Ready-mix has its own catch. Many suppliers charge a short-load fee when an order falls below their minimum, so a 1.5-yard truck can cost much more per yard than a full load. Get a quote for your exact yardage before choosing. Bags also make sense for work that can be poured in sections over several days, like fence posts or a series of small pads.</p>

    <h2>Thickness drives the order more than size</h2>
    <p>Going from 4 to 5 inches adds 25% more concrete; going to 6 inches adds 50%. In the table, the same 12 × 20 ft driveway pad needs 4.07 yards at 5 inches and 4.89 yards at 6. Patios and walkways are commonly poured at 4 inches. Driveways, slabs under vehicles, and anything in freeze-thaw country often call for more. Local code or your contractor sets that number, not the calculator.</p>

    <h2>Why pours come up short</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>A low subgrade.</strong> If the gravel base is just half an inch low across a 4-inch slab, the slab actually needs 12.5% more concrete. Measure depth inside the finished forms at several points and enter the average.</li>
      <li><strong>Thickened edges and footings.</strong> Many slabs are deeper at the perimeter. Calculate the edge as its own strip and add it.</li>
      <li><strong>Measuring the plan instead of the forms.</strong> Forms rarely end up exactly on the drawing. Measure after they are set.</li>
      <li><strong>No waste allowance.</strong> Spillage, form bulge, and concrete left in the chute and wheelbarrow add up. Keep 5 to 10% waste; running out mid-pour leaves a cold joint that is weaker and visible.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Mixed units are a common slip too: the length is in feet, but thickness is usually measured in inches. The calculator converts each field separately, so enter 4 in, not 0.4 ft.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Concrete questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How many 80 lb bags of concrete make a cubic yard?</summary><p>About 45, at a typical yield of 0.60 cu ft per bag (27 ÷ 0.60). With 60 lb bags it is about 60, and with 40 lb bags about 90. Check the yield printed on your brand's bag.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How much concrete do I need for a 10 × 10 slab?</summary><p>At 4 inches thick, a 10 × 10 ft slab is 1.23 cubic yards before waste and about 1.36 yards with 10% waste. Order 1.5 yards from a ready-mix supplier, or buy about 62 bags of 80 lb mix.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Should I round up my ready-mix order?</summary><p>Yes. Suppliers sell in quarter-yard steps, and a 2nd delivery for a small shortfall is expensive and risks a cold joint. Round up to the next quarter yard after adding waste.</p></details>
  </section>
